Adjudicated 2026-09-17. First task gated by `fb differential`, and the gate decided it.

  codex-luna            agrees with the script    251 lines, 0 tests, clippy 2, free
  or-luna-pro           agrees with the script    232 lines, 1 test,  clippy 4, $0.20
  or-hy3                DIVERGES                  462 lines
  or-deepseek-v4-flash  DIVERGES                  NO-OP, no file

OR-HY3 IS THE CATCH. It scored PASS with +462 lines -- the MOST in the field, more than either
arm that works -- and never declared `mod eligible;`. The compiler never saw the file. That is
why it built clean and why its test count stayed at baseline while codex-luna's rose. On a
lines-added heuristic it is the best submission in the wave. `unrecognized subcommand 'eligible'`
is the whole story, and no gate we owned before today could produce that sentence.
or-deepseek-v4-flash wrote no file at all and was already NO-OP.

Between the two that agree, the tiebreak was NOT what I first thought. I flagged codex-luna's
`dispatchable_status` -- twenty lines that build a fake Observation with fabricated tests_run: 1
and lines_added: 1, call gate::judge, and are invoked exactly once with a literal
Measurement::observed(true), so the function can only return true -- and then found or-luna-pro
does the same thing at its line 211. N-of-N: the fault is my SPECIFICATION, which told both arms
"use farmerbob_core::gate::{judge, Observation, Verdict} for any pass/fail verdict". Eligibility
is not a run verdict and Observation's fields are meaningless for it, so both arms fabricated
values to comply. or-luna-pro's version at least passes the real decision through; codex-luna's
is a constant. That difference is marginal and it is not what decided this.

What decided it is Status::Other(Option<String>). or-luna-pro keeps the registry's actual value
inside the variant and renders the absent case as "None", matching the Python; codex-luna
collapses unknown-status and no-status into one variant and recovers the string separately. Both
happen to print the right thing today. One models the distinction and one restores it.

THE DIFFERENTIAL FOUND TWO REAL BUGS IN THE WINNER, AFTER MERGING, BY BEING RUN PAST ITS
DECLARED CASES. Neither was reachable by any other check we own:

1. `${price_in}` was missing from the redundant-route message. Shell prints "$0.2/1.2", the port
   printed "/1.2". price_in is bound and used in the guard above, so nothing warns; it builds,
   lints and tests clean, and prints the wrong sentence to a human. Caught only because I ran the
   comparison over or-luna-pro itself, which was not one of the five declared cases.

2. The registry path was `Path::new("sources.toml")` -- RELATIVE. The script reads an absolute
   "$repo/sources.toml". From any other directory the port answered "cannot read registry" for
   every arm, which would have benched the entire fleet at once. It fails CLOSED, which is the
   one mercy: an unreadable registry refuses everything rather than permitting everything, so it
   could never have caused a dispatch that should not have happened.

   The first fix was wrong too. crate::paths::repo() falls back to the CURRENT DIRECTORY, so it
   reproduced the bug it was meant to remove and the check still passed from the repo root. Now
   $FB_REPO, else walk up for sources.toml, else refuse loudly.

Both fixes verified across all 25 registry arms plus an unregistered name: 0 divergences, run
fresh against the script, never against an artefact. 1087 workspace tests pass, clippy back to
its pre-existing 9.

The lesson is about the gate, not the arm: A DIFFERENTIAL SEES ONLY THE CASES IT IS GIVEN. Five
declared cases passed a port with two real bugs in it. or-luna-pro has since been added to the
declared set, but the general point stands and is worth more than this task -- the case list is
now the thing to attack, and the registry itself is the obvious generator for a predicate like
this one.

Not merged from codex-luna, and it cost nothing, which is the uncomfortable part of the Pareto
story: the free arm and the paid arm produced behaviourally identical, equally correct ports, and
the paid one won on a design detail worth very little. or-luna-pro was parked as a redundant paid
route earlier the same day. Parking governs future dispatch, not work already paid for.
